WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.230 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  these past few months have been proven  to be one of the most challenging starts  to the school year for millions of  students across the country  so when i heard about this amazing  elementary school teacher going above  and beyond for his students  to help make learning a little easier  well we knew we had to meet him  here he is say hello to alejandro dias  granados  from eton elementary school  in washington d.c okay  your story is so incredible like tell us  about your school first of all yeah so  eton elementary is  a school in washington d.c it is in ward  7 northeast d.c  this community has been underserved for  several years  however this community is filled with  such cultural wealth so i like to  describe it as a culturally wealthy  community although it is a title one  school  and majority of the students are  receiving free and reduced lunch but  it is such a loving and caring community  you know down from the the parents to  the teachers the administration  everyone in that community really  pitches in to make sure that  um our students get what they need and  you know eitan is such a beacon of hope  in that community  i think right now when just all bets are  off  uh everybody is sort of  everybody is really figuring out  district by district  city by city the rules like your school  is not open right now you're still  virtual  right where my daughter's school is open  and trying it  and um i felt you know scared i was very  nervous and  you just don't know what is right and  what is wrong and  everybody has a different sort of order  and plan  and in the fact that yours is virtual  what has been your personal mission to  help the students succeed right now  yeah so my personal mission always has  been since when i  uh started at eitan is to give my  students access to a variety of people  programs resources and connections  to and really enhance their learning  experience so it's been a challenge  um you know with the pandemic starting  and teachers really trying to  do everything that they can the teachers  at our school have been amazing to  really trying to connect with students  whether it's through facetime or twitter  instagram or any sort of way that we can  connect with students  that's what we've really been trying and  you know and it's worked out pretty well  for us but you know it is a huge  challenge  do you think any of your students or the  other staff members might be watching  right now  um maybe perhaps we are closed from  school today so they could  possibly be watching i'm not sure you  know what i actually know they are  because they're right here in our  virtual audience  oh my gosh harmony jalen  aiden thank you guys so much for coming  here  like give the shout outs and support  and um i just love when we i i love that  you said the word community  because i do feel like as we're trying  to reinvent the wheel  um and i look back at pictures of 1918  and 1919 and  you know i watch a lot of pbs and like  people are doing outdoor schools they're  doing virtual schools and  some people are podding and hiring one  teacher  and and going that way some schools are  going back and  there sort of is no rules um which is a  little anarchy and scary and  intimidating and at the same time it's  an incredible opportunity to reinvent  the rules  and i just think that you and your  community are doing such a phenomenal  job because everybody's sort of tasked  to do things differently  and the way that you're doing it is so  inspiring to us

